# Post-Task Learning Notes

## Principle

**Capture wide, prune later.** The goal is to never miss a transferable insight
because the agent was being conservative in the moment. Filtering happens
during periodic `/review-learnings` passes, not at write time.

## When to Trigger

After completing **any implementation task** (fix, feature, refine, removal,
migration, test work, infra work — anything that changed files):

| Tier | Briefing required | Persistence required |
|-----|----|----|
| Tier 3 | Yes | Yes — every distinct item |
| Tier 2 | Yes | Yes — every distinct item |
| Tier 1 | Yes (≥ 1 bullet) | Yes when ≥ 1 non-trivial bullet |

For pure Tier 1 changes (typo, copy tweak, single import) where there is
genuinely nothing to learn, write a 1-line briefing stating that explicitly
and skip persistence. Do not skip the briefing.

## What to Include (Inline in Chat)

Append a `---` separator after the task report, then a `### Supervisor's
Briefing` section with **2–5 bullets**. Each bullet is one of:

| Tag | Purpose |
|---|-----|
| **Pattern** | Architectural/code pattern used and why it fits here |
| **Concept** | Dart/Flutter/Riverpod concept worth understanding deeper |
| **Tradeoff** | Why X was chosen over Y (and when Y would be better) |
| **Pitfall** | A common mistake in this area and how to spot it |
| **Debugging** | How the root cause was found — the reasoning chain |
| **Tooling** | A build/test/CLI tool detail worth remembering |
| **API** | A server/contract detail (shape, status, error) worth pinning |
| **Testing** | Widget/controller/integration test pattern worth reusing |
| **Perf** | Rebuild/dispose/render insight |

## Depth Rules

- Match explanation length to concept complexity. Simple = 1–2 sentences.
  Multi-layered = short paragraph.
- Assume the reader is a senior Flutter developer (5+ years). Skip obvious
  things; explain the non-obvious *why*.
- Never pad. If two bullets cover it, do not write five.
- If the same insight applies in two categories, pick the primary one — do
  not duplicate across categories. Cross-references belong in a sub-bullet.

## Persistence — Default On

After writing the inline briefing, **invoke the `post-task-learning` skill
unconditionally** for any Tier 2–3 task and for any Tier 1 task with at least
one non-trivial bullet. The skill is the only writer to
`.cursor/docs/LEARNING_LOG.md`.

The skill is permissive at write time:

- It writes **every distinct bullet** from the briefing.
- It dedupes only against an **exact-concept** match in the log (same idea,
  not just same words).
- It does **not** filter for "obvious" or "hyper-specific" — leave that for
  `/review-learnings`.

## Required Metadata Per Entry

Each log entry must include:

- ISO date `[YYYY-MM-DD]`
- One-line title
- The explanation (same as inline)
- `Context:` line with the trigger — GitHub issue (`#NNN`) or short task name
- Optional `Tier:` line when assigned
- Optional `Files:` line listing the 1–3 most relevant paths touched

## Format Example

```markdown
---

### Supervisor's Briefing

- **Pattern**: `@Riverpod(keepAlive: true)` was used because the provider caches data across tab switches. Without `keepAlive`, navigating away disposes the provider and triggers a re-fetch on return.
- **Concept**: `ref.invalidate()` vs `ref.refresh()` — invalidate marks the provider dirty (rebuilds lazily on next read); refresh forces an immediate rebuild and returns the new value.
- **Pitfall**: Calling `ref.read(provider.notifier).someMethod()` inside `build()` works once but won't react to state changes. Always `ref.watch()` in build, `ref.read()` in callbacks.
```

And the resulting log entries (one per bullet, into the appropriate category):

```markdown
- **[2026-05-21]** `@Riverpod(keepAlive: true)` for tab-survival caches — …
  - *Context: #123 / Tier 2 / `lib/presentation/home/home_controller.dart`*
```
